//
// Phase C2 + D1 + E1 operator gate: ffn.
//
// Staged up-projection, GeLU, down-projection as five launches in one ELF.
// Both GEMMs take their method and tiles from kernel_registry per shape,
// including the memory-tile accumulation depth iron calls down_proj_depth; the
// activation is the ported ffn_gelu_bf16, linked from encoder.cc's FFN half.
//
// Runs opcheck.py on real NPU2 against an FP32 reference: np.isclose over the
// full output at rtol=1.6e-2, zero permitted mismatches. The reference uses the
// TANH approximation, matching the kernel and HuggingFace's gelu_pytorch_tanh
// -- iron's oracle calls torch's erf-form default, whose difference hides at
// iron's 4e-2 tolerance and does not hide at this one.
//
// The reference is FP32 end to end and reproduces neither the bf16 staging of
// the up-projection output nor the activation kernel's bf16 intermediates. That
// is deliberate, and it is why this operator's absolute error is above a single
// GEMM's; the tolerance is sized to the measured worst case, not to the gap.
//
// NPU serialization lives one layer down, not in the recipe below. Device
// access is locked on /tmp/npu.lock, which XRTRunner.run_test holds across
// binary load and dispatch (python/air/backend/xrt_runner.py:292) -- the same
// inode lit.cfg.py's NPU substitution and KernelCache take, so this interlocks
// with every other NPU job in the repository while the peano compiles still
// overlap. Taking the outer /tmp/mlir-air-npu.lock here instead would DEADLOCK:
// the gate command that invokes this suite already holds that inode, and BSD
// flock(2) treats a second open() of the same file as a foreign lock, so the
// nested acquire would block against its own parent until the timeout expires.
//
// Editing this header: lit scans the WHOLE file for its directives, so naming
// one in prose -- even inside a comment -- registers as a second directive and
// leaves the test UNRESOLVED. Describe them, never spell them out.
//
// REQUIRES: ryzen_ai_npu2, peano
//
// RUN: mkdir -p test_npu2_ffn_peano
// RUN: cd test_npu2_ffn_peano
// RUN: make -f %S/Makefile clean
// RUN: make -f %S/Makefile check-ffn PEANO_INSTALL_DIR=%PEANO_INSTALL_DIR | FileCheck %s
//
// Both lines, not just the verdict: the first is XRTRunner's own, the second
// names the operator and shape, so a test wired to the wrong operator fails
// here rather than passing quietly.
// CHECK: PASS!
// CHECK: [opcheck] ffn [2048x1024x3072]: PASS
//
// The second shape is Phase D1's baseline_768 point, and it is the only MIXED-
// METHOD pairing in this operator's ladder: seq 4096 is where the registry puts
// the up-projection on drain and the down-projection on fused-cast. The two
// method names are checked, in order, so a registry change that moved either
// projection fails here with its cause on screen rather than several frames
// inside the stitcher.
//
// Until Phase E1 it was the ONLY point on the ladder where this operator built
// at hidden 768 at all. Both projections take a different tile_n (128 up, 96
// down) everywhere, and two same-method GEMMs at different tile_n declared
// f32_to_bf16_mn_<suffix> twice with different memref types, which stitch_elf
// rejects; 4096 escaped only because the two methods differ there and so the
// suffixes did. E1 made the suffix and the mm.o name functions of
// (method, tile_n) in llms/shared/builders/gemm_builder.py, and the third shape
// below is the consequence.
// CHECK: up-projection 4096x768x3072 (drain, registry
// CHECK: down-projection 4096x3072x768 (fused-cast, registry
// CHECK: PASS!
// CHECK: [opcheck] ffn [4096x768x3072]: PASS
//
// PHASE E1's SECOND LADDER POINT, and the one that could not exist before it.
// Both of seq 64's registry rows resolve to drain -- tile_n 128 for the
// up-projection, 96 for the down -- so this is precisely the same-method,
// different-tile_n pairing that used to fail to parse. Both method names AND
// both tile_n are checked, because "it built" is only evidence that the
// collision is gone if the two really are the same method at different tile_n;
// a registry re-sweep that split them onto two methods would make this shape
// pass for the 4096 row's reason instead, and the pairing under test would have
// silently stopped being tested.
//
// It also runs both GEMMs at herd 2x4 rather than the file-level 8x4: at M = 64
// drain's forced tile_m = 32 admits at most herd_m = 2, and the herd is read off
// the registry row. That is checked too -- it is the shortest M in the file and
// the only place a wrong herd default would surface.
// CHECK: up-projection 64x768x3072 (drain, registry, tile_m=32
// CHECK-SAME: tile_n=128 herd=2x4
// CHECK: down-projection 64x3072x768 (drain, registry, tile_m=32
// CHECK-SAME: tile_n=96 herd=2x4
// CHECK: PASS!
// CHECK: [opcheck] ffn [64x768x3072]: PASS
//
// RUN: make -f %S/Makefile check-ffn-ladder-fault PEANO_INSTALL_DIR=%PEANO_INSTALL_DIR | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=FAULT
//
// The seq-64 point's OWN negative control, and it is here rather than only in
// the driver because of what the shared one does not cover: the generic
// per-operator injection perturbs an operator's FIRST declared shape, which for
// ffn is the Phase C 2048x1024x3072 row. Without this clause the newest point in
// the file would be the only shape anywhere in it that is never injected -- and
// a newly added row is exactly the one whose PASS is least earned, because
// nothing yet shows the comparison can reject it. That gap is what D1 recorded
// for the baseline_768 rows; this is it not being repeated.
//
// The injection line first, so an injection that silently perturbed nothing is
// visible here rather than inferred from the verdict. Then the FAIL, then the
// control's own verdict -- which opcheck.py reports from the completed
// comparison's statistics, not from the exit status, so a missing toolchain or
// an absent NPU cannot masquerade as a caught fault.
// FAULT: [fault-inject] input 0 at (63, 0)
// FAULT: [opcheck] ffn [64x768x3072]: FAIL
// FAULT: NEGATIVE CONTROL: PASS
